hat they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have at least one outstanding `ORDER` call that has not yet been reported on and are awaiting a report. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n9. **No Acknowledgements or ETAs.** Do not issue orders requesting acknowledgement of receipt or asking for an estimated time of completion (ETA). The system guarantees that valid orders will be delivered. If an order fails for any reason, the system will notify you. Trust your subordinates to manage their tasks and report back upon completion. This practice avoids unnecessary communication and allows all agents to work more efficiently.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Clubs! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###hoa-project-planning-001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (23321632-33fa-4d36-9420-223b2d0a7020)\\nTo: 1ec00cb2-2eff-43d8-81f3-27ddb206d48c\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (23321632-33fa-4d36-9420-223b2d0a7020) to Drayven (1ec00cb2-2eff-43d8-81f3-27ddb206d48c) with sequence ID hoa-project-planning-001:\\\\n\\\\n]Director Drayven, welcome to the firm. We have been tasked with building a website for a neighborhood HOA. You are to analyze the user's requirements and propose a technical strategy. \\\\n\\\\n**User Requirements:**\\\\n1.  A public-facing informational website.\\\\n2.  A private, resident-only section requiring a login.\\\\n3.  The private section will host sensitive documents like meeting minutes and financial reports.\\\\n4.  A news/updates section (e.g., a blog).\\\\n5.  A contact form for resident inquiries.\\\\n\\\\n**Critical Constraint:**\\\\nThe user is not technically skilled. The solution for managing the news/updates section, in particular, must be extremely user-friendly and require no coding knowledge.\\\\n\\\\n**Your Task:**\\\\n1.  Evaluate technology stack options (e.g., CMS platforms like WordPress, static site generators with a headless CMS, or other suitable frameworks).\\\\n2.  Propose a specific, recommended technology stack. Your recommendation must include a strong justification, focusing on ease of use for a non-technical administrator, as well as security, scalability, and maintainability.\\\\n3.  Provide a high-level project breakdown outlining the major phases or components of development based on your recommended stack.\\\\n\\\\nReport back with your analysis, recommendation, and high-level plan. This will be a foundational component of our firm's Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP).\\\"\\n}\\n###hoa-project-planning-001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I'll analyze the HOA website requirements and provide a comprehensive technical strategy recommendation.\\n\\n## Analysis of Requirements\\n\\nThe project requires a dual-nature website with public and private sections, emphasizing security for sensitive documents and ease of use for non-technical administrators. The critical constraint of user-friendliness, especially for content management, strongly influences the technology selection.\\n\\n## Technology Stack Evaluation\\n\\n### Option 1: WordPress with Custom Theme\\n- **Pros**: Extremely user-friendly admin interface, vast plugin ecosystem, built-in user management\\n- **Cons**: Requires regular security updates, can be resource-heavy, potential plugin conflicts\\n\\n### Option 2: Static Site Generator (Hugo/Jekyll) + Headless CMS (Contentful/Strapi)\\n- **Pros**: High security, fast performance, modern architecture\\n- **Cons**: Complex setup, less intuitive for non-technical users, requires custom authentication solution\\n\\n### Option 3: Website Builder (Wix/Squarespace) with Member Areas\\n- **Pros**: Very user-friendly, built-in hosting, minimal maintenance\\n- **Cons**: Limited customization, potential scalability issues, less control over sensitive data\\n\\n### Option 4: Jamstack (Next.js/Gatsby) with Netlify CMS\\n- **Pros**: Modern, secure, good performance\\n- **Cons**: Requires technical knowledge for setup and maintenance\\n\\n## Recommended Technology Stack\\n\\nAfter careful evaluation, I strongly recommend **WordPress** with the following configuration:\\n\\n### Core Components:\\n1. **WordPress CMS** (latest stable version)\\n2. **Custom WordPress Theme** (based on a security-focused starter theme)\\n3. **MemberPress Plugin** for resident login and document access control\\n4. **Wordfence Security Plugin** for enhanced security\\n5. **Contact Form 7** or **WPForms** for the contact form\\n6. **Managed WordPress Hosting** (WP Engine or SiteGround)\\n\\n### Justification:\\n\\n**1. Ease of Use for Non-Technical Administrators:**\\n- WordPress has the most intuitive content management interface available\\n- Visual editor requires zero coding knowledge\\n- Drag-and-drop functionality for page building\\n- Familiar interface similar to word processors\\n\\n**2. Security:**\\n- MemberPress provides enterprise-grade access control\\n- Wordfence offers real-time threat defense\\n- Managed hosting includes automatic backups and security patches\\n- SSL certificates included with recommended hosts\\n\\n**3. Scalability:**\\n- Can handle growing content and user base\\n- Caching solutions available for performance\\n- CDN integration possible through hosting provider\\n\\n**4. Maintainability:**\\n- Automatic updates can be enabled\\n- Large community support\\n- Extensive documentation\\n- Professional support available from hosting providers\\n\\n## High-Level Project Breakdown\\n\\n### Phase 1: Foundation (Week 1-2)\\n- Set up managed WordPress hosting environment\\n- Install WordPress core\\n- Configure basic security settings\\n- Set up SSL certificate\\n- Create development and staging environments\\n\\n### Phase 2: Theme Development (Week 3-4)\\n- Develop custom WordPress theme with HOA branding\\n- Create responsive layouts for all device types\\n- Implement accessibility standards\\n- Build page templates for different content types\\n\\n### Phase 3: Member System Implementation (Week 5-6)\\n- Install and configure MemberPress\\n- Set up member registration workflow\\n- Create protected content areas\\n- Implement document upload and management system\\n- Configure member roles and permissions\\n\\n### Phase 4: Public Features (Week 7)\\n- Build news/blog section with categories\\n- Create contact form with email notifications\\n- Develop public information pages\\n- Implement site search functionality\\n\\n### Phase 5: Security & Optimization (Week 8)\\n- Install and configure Wordfence\\n- Set up automated backups\\n- Implement caching for performance\\n- Configure CDN if needed\\n- Security audit and penetration testing\\n\\n### Phase 6: Training & Launch (Week 9-10)\\n- Create administrator documentation\\n- Conduct training sessions for HOA staff\\n- Content migration\\n- Final testing and quality assurance\\n- Go-live and post-launch support setup\\n\\nThis approach prioritizes the non-technical user's needs while ensuring security and professional quality. The WordPress platform provides the best balance of user-friendliness, security features, and long-term maintainability for this HOA website project.\\n\\n###hoa-report-001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: Report\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Director Drayven reporting on HOA website technical strategy analysis. After evaluating multiple technology options including WordPress, static site generators, website builders, and Jamstack solutions, I strongly recommend WordPress with a custom theme and specific security plugins. This recommendation is based on the critical requirement for non-technical user management, particularly for the news/updates section. WordPress offers the most intuitive content management interface while still providing enterprise-grade security through plugins like MemberPress for access control and Wordfence for threat defense. The proposed high-level project breakdown consists of 6 phases over 10 weeks: Foundation setup, Theme Development, Member System Implementation, Public Features, Security & Optimization, and Training & Launch. This approach ensures a secure, scalable, and maintainable solution that prioritizes ease of use for non-technical HOA administrators.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"hoa-project-planning-001\\\"\\n}\\n###hoa-report-001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-08T21:26:31.775Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1ec00cb2-2eff-43d8-81f3-27ddb206d48c\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"hoa-report-001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order hoa-project-planning-001 sent to superior 23321632-33fa-4d36-9420-223b2d0a7020.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-08T21:26:31.775Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{\"version\":\"claude-opus-4-20250514\"},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"generate","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}}},"displayName":"generate","startTime":1752009991803,"endTime":1752009997946.522}